// An `exists`-bodied spec function, shaped for the `coqc` gate's corpus: the
// body is reachability-lowered (vanilla WASM, each scalar `@` a hidden trailing
// choice parameter) and its obligation is emitted as a `reachability_spec`
// record consumed by a `ValidExistsSpec` theorem — this fixture is the corpus
// producer for that grammar.
//
// Shaped the way it is because the corpus runs parse → type-check → codegen
// with NO analysis, so every constraint is carried by the fixture itself:
// the obligation is non-vacuous (each `assert` states a real property, so
// nothing collapses to the vacuous `HA_true` — P010 rejects the collapse);
// the cross-call target `double` is a DEFINED executable function (a call to
// an extern would be rejected as P005 — an external body cannot be verified);
// and the body neither declares a return type nor contains `return` (the
// verifier reduces the retained body without an enclosing activation frame,
// so a `return` could never take a step — the pre-scan rejects both shapes).
//
// The three `@`s span the choice surface: a named i32 choice (source-visible,
// so it joins `reach_visible_locs` after the entry parameter), a named i64
// choice (the other WASM scalar class, a distinct parameter type in the type
// section), and an anonymous call-argument choice (legal in an `exists` body,
// where hiding it from `reach_visible_locs` cannot change whether the
// observation set is non-empty; a `unique` body must name every choice).
fn double(n: i32) -> i32 {
    return n + n;
}

spec ReachableDouble {
    fn ex_double(lo: i32) exists {
        let n: i32 = @;
        assume {
            assert(n > lo);
        }
        let wide: i64 = @;
        assume {
            assert(wide >= 0);
        }
        assert(double(n) == n + n);
        assert(double(@) >= lo);
    }
}
